#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133904 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "282b873c-1ba0-5205-b75f-c58721ffaaff" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Values of a Periodic Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a341a11-ce8b-58fe-beef-6c590edc7b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-41478" :question/problem "![](images/34/34ba4a4dcd6655d68fbc20fb6192354ae755cc7eaf14a2459e9b7012ffe6d9ea.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given that $y=f(x)$ is periodic with period $4,$ what is the value of $f(62)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the period is $4,$ if we add (or subtract) any multiple of $4$ from the input, the output stays the same.\n\nSo, we can keep subtracting $4$ from the input until we get to an input that we see in the graph:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(62) & =f(62-4) \\\\  & =f(62-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =⋯ \\\\  & =f(62-15\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =f(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e22570-10cc-598d-a717-a67ff911634b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51567" :question/problem "![](images/45/4585ab21f7130a45e2ff5871e0fdc66cda5483181033010519781d9725eec36a.png)\n\nThe graph of a periodic function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. What is the period of this function?" :question/worked-solution "Note that if we shift the graph $3$ units left or right, the graph remains unchanged.\n\nOn the other hand, if we shift the graph horizontally by any distance other than $3$ (or a multiple of $3$), we get a different graph.\n\nTherefore, the period of the function is $3.$"}]}
